data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_0b99be24b9f3d9a1006a501055811881958d18636d0d19f9c3","logprobs":[],"output_index":1,"sequence_number":186,"text":"**Foundry Agent Service** is Microsoft’s managed platform for building, deploying, and scaling AI agents in **Microsoft Foundry / Azure AI Foundry**. In short: it gives you a hosted way to run agents that can use models, tools, memory, and external systems without you having to assemble all the runtime plumbing yourself. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/overview))\n\nAt a high level, Microsoft describes it around a few core pieces:\n- a **Responses API** as a single entry point,\n- an **agent runtime** that manages conversations, tool calls, and lifecycle,\n- built-in **tools** like web search, file search, memory, code interpreter, MCP servers, and custom functions,\n- support for multiple models from the Foundry model catalog,\n- plus **observability, identity, and security** features such as tracing, metrics, Entra identity, RBAC, and network isolation. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/overview))\n\nThere are also different ways to use it depending on how much infrastructure you want Microsoft to manage:\n- **Prompt agents**: you define instructions/prompts and Foundry hosts the agent for you.\n- **Hosted agents**: you write agent code using frameworks like Agent Framework, LangGraph, OpenAI Agents SDK, Anthropic Agent SDK, GitHub Copilot SDK, or your own code, package it, and Foundry runs it with managed hosting.\n- **External app + Responses API**: if you already run your own app/runtime, you can still call the API to use Foundry models and tools without moving everything into Foundry hosting. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/overview))\n\nA useful way to think about it is that it’s meant for **agents**, not just chatbots. Microsoft defines an agent as an AI app that can reason over a request and take multi-step actions using tools and data sources, sometimes even without a chat UI. So if you want something that can search files, look things up on the web, call APIs, or automate workflows, Foundry Agent Service is the layer that helps orchestrate that. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/overview))\n\nMicrosoft’s docs also distinguish the **new generally available Agent Service** from the older **“classic” agents** experience. The classic agents documentation says those classic agents are deprecated and will be retired on **March 31, 2027**, and recommends using the newer Microsoft Foundry Agents Service instead. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/agents/how-to/tools-classic/overview))\n\nIf you’re evaluating capabilities, the tool ecosystem is a big part of the value. In the classic docs, Microsoft lists knowledge and action tools such as Azure AI Search, File Search, Microsoft Fabric, web grounding/Bing-based search, Logic Apps, OpenAPI tools, MCP tools, function calling, Azure Functions, and browser automation; the newer service expands the tool catalog further. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/agents/how-to/tools-classic/overview))\n\n**When it’s a good fit**\n- You want a **managed agent backend** instead of building orchestration, scaling, auth, and tracing from scratch.\n- You need agents that use **enterprise data and tools**.\n- You want to switch among supported models without rewriting your whole app.\n- You need **Azure-style security and governance** around agent workloads. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/agents/overview))\n\n**Potential caveats**\n- Some tools, especially Bing/web-grounding related ones, may have **separate costs and compliance considerations**.\n- Microsoft notes that some Bing-grounding features involve data flowing outside the normal Azure compliance boundary, so that matters for regulated workloads. ([learn.microsoft.com](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry-classic/agents/how-to/tools-classic/bing-custom-search))\n\nIf you want, I can also give you one of these next:\n1. a **plain-English explanation** with no Azure jargon,  \n2. a **comparison to OpenAI Assistants / Responses API / LangGraph**, or  \n3. a **hello-world architecture** for using Foundry Agent Service in an app."}

